  📗
    How many bytes more the program holds after *bytes* has been played 2,000
    times, following a warm-up. Counted by the allocator, not by the size of
    the process: that stays up once memory has been used, and a leak that fits
    into what earlier tests freed would not show in it. The count is exact
    because the test program runs without the allocator's cache of freed
    blocks for each thread (the build sets GLIBC_TUNABLES for it): a block in
    that cache is counted as in use. With the cache every scenario wandered
    by kilobytes, so there were 20,000 repetitions and a limit of 32 KB;
    without it every scenario measures 0, and a tenth of the repetitions with
    a limit of one kilobyte finds a smaller leak in a tenth of the time.
